  "semantic_checklist": [
    {
      "id": "C01",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The attached printed pages 4–5 contain equation (1.10), conservation across discontinuities, normalized cell averages, and the neighboring-cell workflow following (1.11). Printed pages 1 and 3 supply the real vector and hyperbolicity context.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation concerns vector conservation laws, normalized cell averages, and interface problems using adjacent states.",
      "reasoning": "The selected passage and inherited context are identifiable from the supplied primary-source renderings. Source-byte hashes were supplied, not independently recomputed."
    },
    {
      "id": "C02",
      "status": "fail",
      "source_evidence": "The source uses real m-component states and neighboring grid cells without specifying an integer index domain, a global state domain for hyperbolicity, or a method structure.",
      "translation_evidence": "The binders require integer-indexed grids, an everywhere differentiable and everywhere hyperbolic flux on all of R^m, and a method carrying a certified solver.",
      "reasoning": "Real vector semantics match, but the bundled objects impose additional domain and applicability restrictions."
    },
    {
      "id": "C03",
      "status": "fail",
      "source_evidence": "The passage describes solving the neighboring-state Riemann problem and using its information to compute a numerical flux and update cell averages.",
      "translation_evidence": "A solver is universally supplied as part of the method, while the existential solved family is not equated to that solver's outputs. Certificates quantify all real temporal endpoints.",
      "reasoning": "The translation changes both the dependence on the supplied solver and the explicit scope of solution certification."
    },
    {
      "id": "C04",
      "status": "fail",
      "source_evidence": "Smoothness is required for deriving the differential equation, while integral conservation is asserted to remain applicable to discontinuous solutions.",
      "translation_evidence": "The method already supplies solutions satisfying all-time rectangle identities and trace integrability; its domain must contain every actual adjacent pair.",
      "reasoning": "The translation avoids classical differentiability of discontinuous solutions, but assumes substantial solution certification and domain conditions absent from the selected passage."
    },
    {
      "id": "C05",
      "status": "fail",
      "source_evidence": "Section 1.2 describes numerical fluxes approximating correct edge fluxes from approximate cell averages. Equation (1.10) is an instantaneous integral balance.",
      "translation_evidence": "The flux map is arbitrary apart from consistency on selected equal-state solver outputs. The conclusion supplies a rectangle identity and an algebraically defined update.",
      "reasoning": "The approximation role and the stated instantaneous balance are not established by the translated conclusions. The qualitative similarity and characteristic-wave discussion is also absent."
    },
    {
      "id": "C06",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Cell averages divide spatial integrals by cell volume; conservation uses the physical flux of the state.",
      "translation_evidence": "D004, D005, D023, D027, D029, and D097 explain normalization, positive lengths, oriented Lebesgue integrals, and solution certification. The ledger explains the remaining algebraic and analytic structures.",
      "reasoning": "The supplied translation interprets the relevant operators substantively rather than relying on names. This establishes interpretability, not equivalence of its rectangle definition with the source law."
    },
    {
      "id": "C07",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.10) uses a time derivative; section 1.1.2 extends integral conservation to discontinuities without specifying its analytic interpretation.",
      "translation_evidence": "D005 requires an exact rectangle identity for every real pair of times and integrable flux traces.",
      "reasoning": "The rectangle identity gives absolute continuity of interval mass and the derivative balance almost everywhere. Equivalence with the source's intended discontinuous interpretation cannot be resolved from these pages."
    },
    {
      "id": "C08",
      "status": "fail",
      "source_evidence": "Information from solving the neighboring-state problem is used to compute a numerical flux for updating approximate cell averages.",
      "translation_evidence": "The chosen certified solutions need not be method.solve outputs. Unew is defined by an algebraic expression without an approximation relation to evolved averages.",
      "reasoning": "The roles of the specified solver, chosen solutions, and evolved numerical quantities are insufficiently connected."
    },
    {
      "id": "C09",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.10) has left flux minus right flux; cell averages are normalized; equation (1.11)'s workflow uses Q_(i-1) on the left and Q_i on the right.",
      "translation_evidence": "The rectangle integrand is F(q(a,tau)) minus F(q(b,tau)); U uses inverse cell length; interface i uses U(i-1), U(i). The update subtracts H(i+1)-H(i).",
      "reasoning": "Normalization, neighboring offsets, and flux orientation agree. The particular update formula is an additional construction, assessed separately."
    },
    {
      "id": "C10",
      "status": "fail",
      "source_evidence": "The source describes finite volume grids and hyperbolic equations generally, without requiring globally admissible real states or bi-infinite cell indexing.",
      "translation_evidence": "Laws are globally differentiable and hyperbolic on R^m; cells range over Z; certified solutions extend to every real time.",
      "reasoning": "These are substantive specializations and extensions of the represented setting, not established nonvacuous strengthening of the selected source result."
    },
    {
      "id": "C11",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.11) leaves x=0 unspecified. Conservation at discontinuities does not specify endpoint traces or exceptional times.",
      "translation_evidence": "Strict-half-line initial conditions leave zero unspecified and positive widths exclude zero denominators. Conservation uses point evaluations at all endpoints with temporal trace integrability.",
      "reasoning": "Jump-point and cell-degeneracy conventions agree. The treatment of discontinuous endpoint traces and exceptional times needs adjudication."
    },
    {
      "id": "C12",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The inherited setting includes scalar constant-coefficient laws and permits ordinary positive-width cells.",
      "translation_evidence": "Positive dimension and widths exclude empty-state and zero-denominator shortcuts; the theorem assumes a certified method.",
      "reasoning": "The assumptions have nontrivial instances: a scalar zero-flux law admits stationary two-state solutions, a one-element information type, and zero numerical flux. However, many conclusions simply unpack supplied certificates or define witnesses; this does not establish the source's broader numerical claims."
    },
    {
      "id": "N01",
      "status": "fail",
      "source_evidence": "The workflow runs from a neighboring-state Riemann solve through information and numerical flux to a cell-average update.",
      "translation_evidence": "I=E(solved) and H=N(I) preserve part of this chain, but solved need not be the specified solver's output, and Unew has only an algebraic definition.",
      "reasoning": "The construction captures an abstract pipeline but does not fully preserve execution by the supplied method or connect its result to the source's flux approximation."
    },
    {
      "id": "N02",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Each component of q represents a conserved density in R^m.",
      "translation_evidence": "The ledger specifies componentwise vector operations and finite-dimensional vector-valued Lebesgue integration.",
      "reasoning": "Component semantics agree. No source norm estimate is being replaced by a different norm convention."
    },
    {
      "id": "N03",
      "status": "not-applicable",
      "source_evidence": "The selected passage provides no quantified error or stability theorem.",
      "translation_evidence": "No forward-error, backward-error, stability, or convergence estimate is asserted.",
      "reasoning": "There is no specific error or stability notion to compare; missing qualitative approximation linkage is assessed under C05 and N04."
    },
    {
      "id": "N04",
      "status": "fail",
      "source_evidence": "Section 1.2 explicitly distinguishes approximate cell averages and numerical fluxes from the correct fluxes they approximate.",
      "translation_evidence": "U is the exact average of a supplied field. H is an arbitrary extracted output subject only to limited equal-state consistency, and Unew is an algebraically defined vector sequence.",
      "reasoning": "Exact initialization can be legitimate, but no represented relation gives H or Unew the stated approximation roles."
    },
    {
      "id": "N05",
      "status": "not-applicable",
      "source_evidence": "The selected pages contain no asymptotic expansion, remainder estimate, or limiting numerical assertion.",
      "translation_evidence": "The conclusions are exact identities and existential constructions. The derivative definition is background structure.",
      "reasoning": "No source asymptotic term or approximation order requires comparison."
    },
    {
      "id": "N06",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The source describes solving hyperbolic Riemann problems and says their solutions are typically similarity solutions; it states no unrestricted global existence or uniqueness theorem.",
      "translation_evidence": "An admitted-domain solver carrying all-real-time conservation certificates is assumed. No entropy, uniqueness, or similarity requirement is present.",
      "reasoning": "Avoiding a universal similarity assertion respects the qualifier, but the source does not determine the existence and regularity scope of these stronger certificates."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "translation_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "no",
      "reasoning": "The conditional construction does not establish the source's numerical approximation content. Its arbitrary information and flux maps, with consistency only on specified equal-state solver outputs, need not approximate correct fluxes on unequal states. Its chosen solutions need not be outputs of that solver, and its updated sequence is not related to evolved cell averages. It also assumes rather than establishes the certified conservation properties."
    },
    "source_implies_translation": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "The source does not specify the analytic interpretation needed to identify its discontinuous integral law with all-real-time rectangle certification. It also does not select the translated method structure or explicit update formula. Much of the conditional translation follows by unpacking its own assumptions and defining witnesses, so the presence of extra structure alone is not a rigorous counterexample to this implication. The source-to-translation correspondence requires adjudication of the analytic interpretation and permissible scope of a formal realization of the descriptive workflow."
    }
  },
  "classification": "undetermined",
  "accepted": false,
  "requires_adjudication": true,
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "unresolved-conservation-interpretation",
      "source_evidence": "Printed pages 4–5 give the instantaneous equation (1.10) and assert continued integral conservation at discontinuities without defining traces or exceptional times.",
      "translation_evidence": "D005 requires spatial-slice and temporal-trace integrability and rectangle identities for all real endpoints and times.",
      "impact": "Equivalence is unresolved. The translated identity yields an almost-everywhere derivative statement, which cannot silently be identified with every possible interpretation of the source."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "missing-numerical-approximation-link",
      "source_evidence": "Section 1.2 describes numerical flux functions approximating correct fluxes from approximate cell averages.",
      "translation_evidence": "The extractor and flux map have no accuracy relation on unequal states; Unew is only a flux-difference expression.",
      "impact": "The translation permits pipelines without the approximation role asserted by the source."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "solver-output-dependence",
      "source_evidence": "The paragraph following (1.11) obtains information by solving the adjacent-state Riemann problem.",
      "translation_evidence": "D017 and the restrictions explicitly state that solved(i) need not equal method.solve on that problem. Equal-state consistency concerns only method.solve outputs.",
      "impact": "The stated consistency need not apply to the chosen flux family, even at an equal-state interface."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "conditional-repackaging-and-scope",
      "source_evidence": "The selected discussion supplies general conservation and finite volume context without a certified-method premise, global real-state admissibility requirement, or explicit update equation.",
      "translation_evidence": "The law and method bundle global differentiability, hyperbolicity, admitted-domain solutions, and consistency; conclusions largely unpack these assumptions and define successive functions.",
      "impact": "The result represents a conditional abstract construction with additional restrictions. It cannot be accepted as a stronger source theorem merely because its certificates and formula are more explicit."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The primary-source images support the supplied extraction. The translation preserves real vector states, normalization, flux orientation, neighboring indices, and the unspecified jump-point value. It does not preserve the complete numerical approximation meaning, and its analytic replacement of the discontinuous integral law remains unresolved. Acceptance is therefore unwarranted; adjudication is required for the unresolved implication and semantic checks."
}
